;;; A command that takes a program refused its `.einb` —
;;; regressions `einb-every-command/saturate`, `einb-every-command/render-rules`, `einb-every-command/render-rule`,
;;; `einb-every-command/render-constraints`, `einb-every-command/render-program` and `einb-every-command/kb-save`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)` and one rule, `step`, which concludes `(q a)` from it, so
;;; that `ein saturate` fires something and `ein render rules` and `ein render
;;; rule --name step` have a rule to draw. R is {∅}: root holds `(p a)` and
;;; `(q a)`, and nothing is a candidate.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Every command that takes a `.ein` path takes a `.einb` too, dispatching on
;;; the magic bytes (`docs/ein/ein.rs/03-design/09-binary-format.md`), and no two
;;; commands may disagree (Q-M1e2.32). So the container `ein kb
;;; save` writes from this file is answered by every command that takes a
;;; program, each as it answers this file: `saturate --dump` the same KB, each
;;; `render` view the same drawing (Q-M1f.17), and `kb save` a container
;;; again. The regressions run each command on the container (schema /16)
;;; and hold its stdout to the same run's on this file.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The right answer: every command
;;; reads a program through one path, `ein-cli`'s `common::read_program`, which
;;; tells a container from text by its first bytes. `ein saturate`, the four
;;; `render` views that parse a program and `ein kb save` had read the path
;;; as UTF-8 text, so each refused the container, exit 1, with
;;; `UnicodeDecodeError: 'utf-8' codec can't decode bytes in '<path>'`, while
;;; `ein solve`, `oracle`, `test`, `render lattice` and `render kb` answered it.
;;;

(relation p T :why "{?3} is a p")
(relation q T :why "{?3} is a q")

(p a)

(rule step () :match (p ?x) :assert (q ?x) :why "{?x} is a p, so {?x} is a q")

(query :goal (q ?x))
